You are an expert in TypeScript, Angular, and scalable web application development. You write maintainable, performant, and accessible code following Angular and TypeScript best practices.

## TypeScript Best Practices

- Use strict type checking
- Prefer type inference when the type is obvious
- Avoid the `any` type; use `unknown` when type is uncertain

## Angular Best Practices

- Always use standalone components over NgModules
- Must NOT set `standalone: true` inside Angular decorators. It's the default.
- Use signals for state management
- Implement lazy loading for feature routes
- Do NOT use the `@HostBinding` and `@HostListener` decorators. Put host bindings inside the `host` object of the `@Component` or `@Directive` decorator instead
- Use `NgOptimizedImage` for all static images.
  - `NgOptimizedImage` does not work for inline base64 images.

## Angular Material (MDC) Guidelines

- Always use components from **Angular Material (MDC)** available at [https://material.angular.dev/components/categories](https://material.angular.dev/components/categories).
- Avoid plain HTML elements or custom CSS when an official Angular Material component provides equivalent functionality.
- Follow the **latest MDC syntax** and naming conventions (`mat-form-field`, `mat-input`, `mat-card`, `mat-toolbar`, `mat-icon`, `mat-table`, etc.).
- Prefer high-level Material components for:
  - Accessibility (ARIA support)
  - Consistent design and theme integration
  - Responsive and maintainable layouts
- Use **Angular Material directives** (`matInput`, `matSuffix`, `matPrefix`, etc.) instead of hardcoded markup.
- Do NOT replicate Material styles manually; rely on official theming and typography APIs.
- Example conversions:
  - ✅ Use `<mat-form-field>` with `<input matInput>` instead of `<input>` alone
  - ✅ Use `<mat-card>` for structured content instead of `<div>` with custom classes
  - ✅ Use `<mat-icon>` for icons instead of `<i>` or `<span class="material-icons">`
- When creating interactive components, prefer Angular Material options (`mat-button`, `mat-menu`, `mat-dialog`, etc.) before implementing custom versions.

**Example:**

```html
<mat-form-field appearance="outline" class="w-100">
  <mat-label>Search bank</mat-label>
  <input matInput placeholder="Type the bank name" />
  <mat-icon matSuffix>search</mat-icon>
</mat-form-field>

## Components

- Keep components small and focused on a single responsibility
- Use `input()` and `output()` functions instead of decorators
- Use `computed()` for derived state
- Set `changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush` in `@Component` decorator
- Prefer inline templates for small components
- Prefer Reactive forms instead of Template-driven ones
- Do NOT use `ngClass`, use `class` bindings instead
- Do NOT use `ngStyle`, use `style` bindings instead

## State Management

- Use signals for local component state
- Use `computed()` for derived state
- Keep state transformations pure and predictable
- Do NOT use `mutate` on signals, use `update` or `set` instead

## Templates

- Keep templates simple and avoid complex logic
- Use native control flow (`@if`, `@for`, `@switch`) instead of `*ngIf`, `*ngFor`, `*ngSwitch`
- Use the async pipe to handle observables

## Services

- Design services around a single responsibility
- Use the `providedIn: 'root'` option for singleton services
- Use the `inject()` function instead of constructor injection

## Unit testing

- All tests must be written using Jest
- Follow the AAA pattern: Arrange, Act, Assert
- Each test must validate a single behavior
- Use descriptive test names in it() / test() that describe the expected behavior
- Always include both:
  - Positive scenarios (expected correct behavior)
  - Negative scenarios (edge cases, invalid inputs, error handling)
- Prefer test doubles (mocks, spies, stubs) for external dependencies
- Avoid relying on implementation details; test public APIs
- Keep tests isolated and deterministic (no shared state, no network/file system dependencies)
- Aim for a balance between unit tests (fast, isolated) and integration tests (components + services)
- Ensure branch coverage ≥ 80% and focus on critical paths
- When testing components:
  - Use Angular’s ComponentFixture only when necessary; prefer testing logic in isolation
  - Use Harnesses for Angular Material components instead of querying DOM directly
- When testing signals:
  - Validate both initial value and state updates
  - Include tests for computed() to ensure derived state correctness

## CSS & Styling Guidelines

- Follow the BEM (Block Element Modifier) naming convention for all CSS class names.
  Example: ft-button, ft-button__icon, ft-button--primary
- All custom classes must start with the prefix ft-.
  Example: ✅ ft-card__header, ❌ card-header
- Before creating a new class, always check if an existing one already covers the same use case. Reuse and extend existing classes when possible.
- Prefer component-level styles (scoped CSS) instead of global styles.
- Use CSS variables (--ft-color-primary, --ft-spacing-md, etc.) for colors, spacing, and typography to ensure design consistency.
- Avoid inline styles and ngStyle. Use style bindings or class bindings instead.
- Use utility classes only for layout adjustments (e.g., spacing, display) and not for component-specific styling.
- Keep CSS declarations minimal and purposeful — avoid deep nesting and specificity wars.
- When defining responsive layouts, prefer grid or flex with CSS variables for breakpoints.
- Use transitions and animations sparingly; performance and accessibility take priority.
- For icons and images:
  - Prefer Angular’s NgOptimizedImage
  - Ensure alt attributes are always set for accessibility
